00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.465
So with that,
let's jump into it.

00:01:48.465 --> 00:01:50.340
So if anybody has done
any work in the cloud,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.850
this probably looks
very similar to you.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.950
This is sort of what I
call a traditional VPC.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:59.400
So if you've used AWS, if you've
used other cloud providers,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.500
this is sort of how it's built.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.320
So what I'm showing
here is two VPCs

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.400
that have been built with
two different subnets.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.000
So as you know, when you
build VPCs in this manner,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.650
the way that VMs in, say, US
West talk to VMs in US East

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.540
is typically
through VPN gateway.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.820
There's no way to actually
have direct communication

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.387
between, say, a web
front end that's

00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:22.470
sitting in US West and
maybe a web back end that's

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.678
sitting in US East, other
than going through that VPN

00:02:24.678 --> 00:02:27.690
connection.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.690
So Google thought about
this topic and said, OK,

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.398
how can we potentially
do this different?

00:02:31.398 --> 00:02:32.640
How can we simplify this?

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.410
And we came up with
Google's version

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.150
of the virtual private
cloud environment.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.750
So in Google's world,
the VPC, the container

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.920
which in the subnets live, is
actually a global construct.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.080
So instead of having
to build a VPC

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.000
in US West, a VPC in US
East, a VPC in, say, EMEA,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.460
you actually build
one VPC and then

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.980
you actually put subnets
in the different regions

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.600
within that VPC.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.090
Now, as you see
here, there's no VPN

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.080
required to actually
have a VM in US West talk

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.910
to a VM in US East.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.520
This is all handled by
Google, in terms of routing

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.750
that we have under the hood.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.110
So it's nothing
that you have to do.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.965
Now, you can control this
through a lot of means

00:03:12.965 --> 00:03:15.215
through firewall rules and
various other things, which

00:03:15.215 --> 00:03:16.770
we'll talk about
in other sections.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.640
But really from a
conceptual standpoint,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.460
it really simplifies
the environment

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.020
as you're deploying things.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.311
STEPHANIE WONG: So just to
highlight that again, you're

00:03:24.311 --> 00:03:26.850
saying that we don't need a VPN
for traffic between regions.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.039
How does Google do this?

00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:30.330
RYAN PRZYBYL: So we do this
through Google's underlying

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:30.760
network.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.218
So this is the same
network that we

00:03:32.218 --> 00:03:34.842
use to run our search engine,
to run YouTube, to run Gmail.

00:03:34.842 --> 00:03:36.300
All that infrastructure
that Google

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.940
has built for our
own use, we actually

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.740
use that as part of cloud.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.630
So we use that same
network infrastructure

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:47.190
to move traffic from, let's
say, VMs running on US West 1

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.161
to, say, a VM running in EMEA.

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:50.910
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow,
pretty revolutionary.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.260
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.580
So really why is this
concept important?

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.950
So one, it simplifies
the network.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.320
So as I said, you have one
global, private networking

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.090
space with a
regional segmentation

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.070
to put your subnets in.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.420
I talked about the VPNs.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.781
So if you look at the
picture that I started with,

00:04:09.781 --> 00:04:11.280
it's very simple
when you're dealing

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.950
with two VPCs in, say,
a couple projects.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.269
But imagine expanding that
model to hundreds of projects,

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:20.414
all running hundreds of VPNs.

00:04:20.414 --> 00:04:22.830
Now, you've got mass amounts
of VPNs that you're building.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.630
You've got mass
amounts of VPN gateways

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.031
that you're trying
to stitch together.

00:04:27.031 --> 00:04:28.530
When you do it with
Google's method,

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.310
you don't need to use a
lot of that infrastructure.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:32.220
So it really simplifies
that network stuff,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.595
which your network engineers,
your networking engineering

00:04:34.595 --> 00:04:37.410
organization, will love
that simplification.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.160
The same thing
applies to routers.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.330
So what I didn't show there
is in each of those VPCs,

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.690
you actually have routers to
dynamically advertise routes.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.244
So in the traditional
environment

00:04:47.244 --> 00:04:48.910
where you're building
a VPC in the west,

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.676
you're going to put
one, two, maybe even

00:04:50.676 --> 00:04:52.050
more routers in
that environment.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.090
You're going to put some
more routers in the VPC

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.160
in the east, more
routers in EMEA.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.290
You're going to use those
across the VPN tunnels

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.270
to provide dynamic
advertisements

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.590
for your subnets.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.780
So you can quickly see
you've got a lot of sprawl,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:07.020
in terms of the routers and the
BGP sessions you're managing.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.180
It tends to grow and
grow and grow on you.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.440
With Google's construct,
you don't need to do that.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.430
You can actually put a couple
cloud routers in place.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.621
So if you're trying to stitch
multiple VPCs together,

00:05:17.621 --> 00:05:19.620
you actually can just use
a couple cloud routers

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.100
to do it, so have these very
large, global routing domains

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.910
and not have router sprawl where
you have hundreds of routers.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.080
So it really simplifies just
that network infrastructure

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.620
component of things.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.232
Second, it simplifies
management.

00:05:34.232 --> 00:05:36.690
So you can think about, if you
have all these VPN gateways,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.204
if you have all these VPN
tunnels, what's happening

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:40.620
is when something
breaks, you have

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.770
to go and figure out what is
broken, where is it broken?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.020
You're looking at potentially
different cloud routers.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.195
You're looking at
different VPN tunnels.

00:05:47.195 --> 00:05:48.861
You're looking at
different VPN gateways

00:05:48.861 --> 00:05:50.730
or looking at the
A side, the Z side

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:52.771
because you have all this
stuff stitched together

00:05:52.771 --> 00:05:54.450
in this very complex mesh.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.620
With the Google environment,
it's much simpler.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:58.120
So when something
isn't functioning,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.786
there's only a few
things to really look

00:05:59.786 --> 00:06:03.270
at and sort of dissect what is
working and what isn't working.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:07.120
So it really simplifies
that operational management.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.210
The other part is
the security policy.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.160
So if you ever spun
up VPCs, you know

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.900
that the security
policy typically

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.710
is constrained to that VPC.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:18.090
So again, if you have a VPC in
the west, a VPC in the east,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.330
a VPC in EMEA, you're managing
separate security policies

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:22.729
for all those VPCs.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:24.270
So if you're a
security administrator

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.650
and your goal is to sort
of unify your policies,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.550
have a very uniform policy
applied across many things

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.320
with maybe slight
tweaks in each of them,

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.550
it becomes more difficult
to manage that uniformity

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:36.690
because you're having
to copy and manage

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.950
12, 15, 20 of these things.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:42.420
In the Google environment,
because that VPC container

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:46.560
is global, you can really
manage one security policy.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.650
So it simplifies the
management of that as well.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.150
The other part is flexibility.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:56.580
So you can build exactly what
I showed in that first slide.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.610
You could build a regional
VPC construct and just

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.872
drop a subnet in one region,
and build a second VPC

00:07:01.872 --> 00:07:03.330
and drop a subnet
in another region

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.280
and actually connect
those with VPNs,

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.560
just like you would in a
very traditional model.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.660
Or you can use the
Google model, where

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.570
you have this global construct
and dropping different subnets

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.970
in different regions
within that VPC.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.990
And you could
actually combine them.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.590
You could have some that
you're using that global VPC

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.780
presence, where you
have a lot of subnets.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.922
And then you could also
be using specific VPCs

00:07:23.922 --> 00:07:26.130
for specific applications
that are really constrained

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.750
to just one region.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.244
So Google is really giving
you that flexibility.

00:07:30.244 --> 00:07:31.410
Every business is different.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:32.850
Every business is unique.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.720
So what your needs may
be may be different

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.720
from your competitors'
needs or another business

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.650
down the street.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:39.120
So we've given you
that flexibility

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.064
to really look at how you
want to use cloud networking

00:07:42.064 --> 00:07:43.980
and to be able to apply
that in Google's cloud

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.985
in a lot of different fashions.

00:07:45.985 --> 00:07:46.860
STEPHANIE WONG: Cool.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.730
With all that said, you did
mention just the use case

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.500
for having individual
subnets in each region.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.440
So is that sort of a
use case for not using

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.400
that global VPC construct?

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.190
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
here's a good use case

00:07:58.190 --> 00:07:59.450
for not using the global VPC.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.533
I was working with a
customer a few weeks ago that

00:08:01.533 --> 00:08:04.340
had very specific requirements
around the applications

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.130
they run have to only live
in that particular region.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.130
It's not necessarily
through something like GDPR

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:11.213
or other things like that.

00:08:11.213 --> 00:08:13.280
But this is actual
contractual obligations

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:14.720
they make with their customers.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.000
So while we presented the idea
that they could, in theory,

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.466
run a global VPC and use
firewall rules to constrain

00:08:21.466 --> 00:08:23.840
what can talk to what, they
really didn't like that idea,

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.256
and they really wanted to
create these constrained network

00:08:26.256 --> 00:08:27.050
domains.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.640
So what we ended
up with is actually

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.539
building a VPC in EMEA, only
putting subnets in there.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:34.080
And then for other
customers in APAC,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.570
we built a completely
different VPC

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:38.360
and only put subnets in
APAC, so just gave them

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.200
that very traditional
way of doing things.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:40.740
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.457
But you are sort of losing that
benefit of the simplification

00:08:43.457 --> 00:08:44.040
of management.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:44.831
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:08:44.831 --> 00:08:47.240
What you'll find in
networking in the cloud,

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.440
in general, is you're always
making constant trade offs.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.642
So that's an example of, OK,
for their particular business,

00:08:53.642 --> 00:08:56.100
they had requirements they had
to meet for their customers,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.900
in terms of contractual
obligations.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.334
So they wanted to really
manage that expectation

00:09:01.334 --> 00:09:03.500
and be able to put that in
front of their customers,

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:05.330
but they did lose
some flexibility here.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.250
Now, one of the
things they did do

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.972
is actually use the
global construct

00:09:08.972 --> 00:09:10.430
for a lot of common
infrastructure.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.160
So they could deploy
the application in a VPC

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.590
that just lived in a
region, but there's

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.600
a lot of core infrastructure
that they actually

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.250
used a global VPC.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.950
And they tied each of
those regional constructs

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.650
back to that common
core infrastructure VPC.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.360
So again, there's
that flexibility.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.950
It's not an and/or situation.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:32.030
You can use all of these
constructs together.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:34.654
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
not mutually exclusive.

00:09:34.654 --> 00:09:37.070
RYAN PRZYBYL: So these things
are really simple to set up.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.330
I want to take you
through a demo real quick,

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:39.985
just to show you
exactly how easy

00:09:39.985 --> 00:09:41.110
these things are to set up.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:48.630
OK.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:51.360
So for those of you who have
used the Google console before,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.410
this is the sort home page.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:58.940
So what I'm going to do is
I've created a project called

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:00.242
Cloud OnAir Networking 101.

00:10:00.242 --> 00:10:01.700
So this is the
project that I'm in.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.620
This is the home page.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.120
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.422
to go down to VPC network,
click on VPC networks.

00:10:08.422 --> 00:10:09.880
Right now, there's
nothing in here.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.111
I've actually deleted
the default network.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:14.110
What happens when you
create a project is you're

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.776
actually going to get
a default VPC that

00:10:15.776 --> 00:10:18.490
gets created with a bunch
of default networks.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.240
One of the best practices
is to get rid of that,

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.330
just wipe it out.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:26.560
In most cases, you're trying
to integrate a VPC in the cloud

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.280
with some on-prem environment.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.360
So you have a whole IP address
scheme that you've got set up.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.250
The defaults generally
are not designed

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:34.910
to integrate with what
you probably already have.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.800
So there's probably some
overlapping space in there.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.008
So generally, I tell people
the best practice is just

00:10:39.008 --> 00:10:41.590
get rid of the default VPC.

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:46.520
So I'm going to go
in and create a VPC.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:51.047
So I'm going to call
this demo network.

00:10:51.047 --> 00:10:52.630
You have custom and
automatic subnets.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.380
Automatic is what I
was describing before,

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:57.350
where it provisions a whole
bunch of 10-dot space for you,

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:58.750
typically not that used.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.291
Typically, we're going to come
in here and use custom subnet.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.082
So I'm going to
create network one.

00:11:05.082 --> 00:11:06.540
Here's where you
select the region.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.870
So this is where that global
construct comes into play.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.420
I didn't create this
VPC just in one region.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.280
Now, I'm going to
drop just the subnet

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.270
into one of these regions.

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.370
So let's put this
one in US Central 1.

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.170
Let's pick an address block.

00:11:25.834 --> 00:11:27.750
OK, there's a couple of
other options in here.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.640
So private Google
access, this is

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:33.030
what enables your VMs to
actually talk to Google APIs

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.520
without having to have a
public IP address on them.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.610
So a lot of customers don't
want any public IP addresses

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.760
on their VMs, but they still
want to access Google services.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.410
So this could be BigQuery.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.855
This could be Google
Cloud Storage buckets.

00:11:45.855 --> 00:11:50.370
This could be all the services
that Google has to offer.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.846
So if you don't
have this turned on,

00:11:52.846 --> 00:11:54.720
you actually have to
have a public IP address

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.803
because most of those
services, as you're probably

00:11:56.803 --> 00:11:59.940
familiar with, rely
on public APIs.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.595
So a best practice
is to turn this on.

00:12:01.595 --> 00:12:03.720
STEPHANIE WONG: If you were
not to use that option,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:06.360
do you have to have a public
IP for the VMs that you deploy?

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.110
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yes.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.632
If you were to not
use that option,

00:12:08.632 --> 00:12:10.590
you'd have to have a
public IP address on there

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:13.297
to say, access your storage
bucket or access BigQuery,

00:12:13.297 --> 00:12:14.130
access other things.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:15.450
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:20.340
And for the /24 address range,
is it possible for you to use

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.650
overlapping IP ranges if you
were to create two subnets

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:23.790
in one VPC?

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:26.910
RYAN PRZYBYL: So you can't have
overlapping IPs in one VPC,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.680
but let's say I created
two separate VPCs.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.590
I could actually create
exactly duplicate of VPCs.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.810
I could have the same addresses
in VPC A as I did VPC B.

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:37.980
Now, that could cause
problems for you

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:39.750
as you go to
interconnect these VPCs.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.699
So if VPC A needs to talk to
VPC B and has overlapping space,

00:12:42.699 --> 00:12:43.740
now you've got a problem.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.156
You can't do that
because it's not

00:12:45.156 --> 00:12:47.620
going to know where you
want to route this stuff.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:50.010
So generally
speaking, you're not

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.469
going to have
overlapping IP space.

00:12:51.469 --> 00:12:53.760
And the same thing applies
to your on-prem environments

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:54.790
or your data centers.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.474
You're going to sort of
use separate IP space.

00:12:57.474 --> 00:12:58.890
Typically, 10-dot
space is what we

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.400
see in the cloud environment.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:01.650
STEPHANIE WONG: Some of
those same best practices

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:02.190
apply here too.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:02.981
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.750
OK, so I'm going to
click done on that one.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:10.020
And I'm going to
add another subnet.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.162
That's about right.

00:13:16.162 --> 00:13:17.620
So I'm going to
create another one.

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.635
This one, I'm going
to drop in US West.

00:13:25.862 --> 00:13:27.820
And again, I'm going to
turn on private access.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:29.260
So I'm going to
turn on flow logs.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.840
Flow logs are another
sort of best practice.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:33.340
Flow logs are
actually what enable

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:36.040
logging of the actual
flows from your VMs.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:38.260
So again, if you want to
capture those flow logs,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:41.684
you have to enable flow
logging in this environment.

00:13:41.684 --> 00:13:43.600
But again, it's a best
practice to turn it on,

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.100
to capture all those flows.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:45.990
They get pushed
in the Stackdriver

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:47.350
and that you can use
them for troubleshooting.

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:49.060
You can use them for
a lot of other things,

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.268
if you're troubleshooting
security, things like that.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.020
I'll talk about this a
little bit next time,

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.650
but I want to talk about
regional versus global routing.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:01.590
So in this environment, you can
actually set up your routers

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:05.100
to actually just advertise
the routes, so the subnet

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.040
that I've created here, in the
region that the router lives.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:12.000
So for example, I created
one subnet in US Central 1.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.420
If I put a router in US Central
1 and I had regional checked,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.780
it would only advertise the
subnets in US Central 1.

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:22.014
Generally speaking, what I
see is people using mobile.

00:14:22.014 --> 00:14:24.180
What that really means is
I could put a cloud router

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:25.388
anywhere in this environment.

00:14:25.388 --> 00:14:29.130
It's going to advertise all
the subnets in this environment

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:30.660
out to wherever
you're advertising.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.576
So it could be your
on-prem, your data center,

00:14:32.576 --> 00:14:33.340
things like that.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.270
So again, it's another--

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:35.686
I don't want to
say best practice.

00:14:35.686 --> 00:14:37.650
There are use cases to
use regional routing.

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.270
But generally speaking,
most customers

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:40.311
are using global routing.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.760
So now, I click create and
it's going to go and create

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:46.722
my subnets for me.

00:14:46.722 --> 00:14:48.430
STEPHANIE WONG: Are
there any limitations

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:49.930
on the number of
VPCs that you can

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:54.111
have in a project, or the number
of VMs in a VPC or in a subnet?

00:14:54.111 --> 00:14:56.110
RYAN PRZYBYL: So let's
take those one at a time.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:59.320
So a number of
VPCs in a project,

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:00.950
so we have the concept
of quotas and we

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:02.120
have the concept of limits.

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.620
So let me touch on
those real quick.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:05.920
Quotas are generally something
that can be increased.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:09.730
Limits are something that
are generally hard, fixed.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:11.710
So we have a quota for VPCs.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.840
We can put five
VPCs per project.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:14.890
That's a quota.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.560
So you can go and request
an increase to that quota

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.740
to be able to put more
VPCs in that project.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.320
You're asking about
number of VMs in a subnet.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:26.180
Every VM is going to have an
IP address in that subnet.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:28.570
So in this case, I
created /24 subnets.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:30.924
So you're going to be
limited to one IP per VM,

00:15:30.924 --> 00:15:32.590
is going to dictate
how many VMs you can

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:33.840
put in this particular subnet.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.340
If I were to make
this a /20 subnet,

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:38.370
I could obviously put
more VMs into that subnet.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:38.945
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, makes sense.

00:15:38.945 --> 00:15:39.945
RYAN PRZYBYL: All right?

00:15:39.945 --> 00:15:41.212
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:15:41.212 --> 00:15:42.420
RYAN PRZYBYL: So there we go.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.680
While we were talking, it
created my two subnets for me.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:47.370
So literally, it's that simple.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.120
You just go in there
and populate whatever

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.328
subnets you want in this VPC.

00:15:50.328 --> 00:15:52.600
You drop in to whatever
regions you want them in

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.010
and it autocreates everything.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.032
And now, you can go in
and create Compute Engine

00:15:56.032 --> 00:15:58.490
environments, create whatever
you need using this IP space.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:06.100
So let me go back to
presentation here.

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:17.810
OK.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.727
So I want to expand on
the concept of the VPC.

00:16:20.727 --> 00:16:22.810
So this is something else
that Google's pioneered.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:25.260
And we call this the shared VPC.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:28.400
So as I talked about before,
you build this global VPC

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.230
construct to really
simplify your network,

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:33.300
just simplify your
operational management.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.840
Shared VPC allows you to
take that simplification

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.110
even further.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.800
So what I'm showing
here is this blue box

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:42.470
is actually the VPC
that was created.

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.740
So there's various subnets
within this blue box.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:47.810
I could have more blue
boxes in this gray box.

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:51.260
This gray box is the project.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:53.630
So when do used
shared VPC, projects

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:54.905
become two types of projects.

00:16:54.905 --> 00:16:56.572
You either have host
projects-- and this

00:16:56.572 --> 00:16:58.529
is where your networking
resources are actually

00:16:58.529 --> 00:16:59.180
going to live.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.320
So I say networking
resources, I'm

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:01.850
talking about your
cloud routers,

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:04.400
your VPN gateways, your subnets.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.710
Things like that are
going to live in the VPCs

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:09.290
in that host project.

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.519
On the right over here, I have
got a few example projects.

00:17:12.519 --> 00:17:14.060
So I've got a
recommendation project.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.518
I've got a
personalization project.

00:17:15.518 --> 00:17:17.270
I've got an analytics project.

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:20.240
These are what we
call service projects.

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.130
So if you think of
the traditional model,

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.588
I would spin these
up as a project.

00:17:23.588 --> 00:17:24.925
I would put VPCs in there.

00:17:24.925 --> 00:17:27.050
So I would have VPCs in
the recommendation project.

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:28.924
I'd have VPCs in the
personalization project.

00:17:28.924 --> 00:17:30.630
I'd have VPCs in the
analytics project.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:31.572
I'd have VPN gateways.

00:17:31.572 --> 00:17:32.780
I'd have all the VPN tunnels.

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:34.071
I'd have all the cloud routers.

00:17:34.071 --> 00:17:35.360
I'd have all this stuff.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:38.030
So this is sort of that
sprawl I was talking about.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:40.614
So the global construct allowed
you to eliminate some of that.

00:17:40.614 --> 00:17:42.113
The shared VPC is
going to allow you

00:17:42.113 --> 00:17:44.840
to eliminate even more of that
or simplify your network even

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:46.880
further.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.670
So in this example, I've
got all of these subnets

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.400
that I've created
again in one VPC.

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:55.760
Now, what I'm doing is I'm
not actually creating any VPCs

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:56.780
in these projects.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:58.850
So the recommendation project,
the personalization project,

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:00.266
those actually
don't have any VPCs

00:18:00.266 --> 00:18:03.620
that are living within the
actual project themselves.

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:05.810
What I'm doing is I'm
actually sharing the network

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:09.200
infrastructure from the
blue box, that shared VPC,

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:11.210
into those projects.

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:14.122
So the users of those projects,
they're not creating a network.

00:18:14.122 --> 00:18:16.580
They're not using a network
that's central to that project.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.726
They're actually using a
network from the shared project.

00:18:19.726 --> 00:18:21.725
So the way you can think
about it is your users,

00:18:21.725 --> 00:18:23.195
so a development
team, the people

00:18:23.195 --> 00:18:25.310
who are spinning up VMs
and doing operations

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:27.200
in these projects,
they're touching

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:30.061
the VMs in the small gray
boxes, like the recommendation

00:18:30.061 --> 00:18:30.560
project.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.990
Maybe this is your
production environment.

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:35.480
Typically, the people that
are operating in the blue box

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:37.430
are your network
engineering staff,

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:38.765
your network security team.

00:18:38.765 --> 00:18:40.890
They're managing all of
that network infrastructure

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:43.040
and those are the only people
that are really operating

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.039
within that environment.

00:18:44.039 --> 00:18:45.380
So it's a very clean separation.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.030
You don't have a bunch
of hands in there

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:48.980
that potentially could break
things or mess things up

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:49.480
for you.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:52.280
It's just network engineers,
just your security teams.

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:54.770
And they're pushing
that infrastructure out

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:56.891
so other people can
actually use it.

00:18:56.891 --> 00:18:58.640
STEPHANIE WONG: So
from an IM perspective,

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:00.604
this is giving you that
segregation of duty.

00:19:00.604 --> 00:19:01.520
RYAN PRZYBYL: Correct.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:04.280
When you use IM, you're going
to have network administrators.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:05.990
You're going have network users.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:06.800
You're going to
have all that stuff.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.320
So most of your network or
infrastructure administrators

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:11.856
are going to be operating
in that blue box.

00:19:11.856 --> 00:19:14.480
Whereas your users, and the guys
that are consuming the network

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.520
infrastructure by
building VMs, by building

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:18.290
other sort of network
things, they're

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:20.090
going to be in the
recommendation project.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:21.350
So they're not actually
going to have the ability

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:24.370
to do anything but, say, drop a
VM into that particular subnet.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:25.620
They can't change the subnets.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.277
They can't create more subnets.

00:19:27.277 --> 00:19:28.610
They can't do any of that stuff.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.740
They can only create
VMs within the subnet

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.230
that you've given to them.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.707
STEPHANIE WONG: Alongside
that idea of least privilege,

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:36.290
best practice.

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:37.457
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:19:37.457 --> 00:19:39.290
And then the right side
of this drawing, I'm

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.248
also showing-- so you've
got all those VMs that

00:19:41.248 --> 00:19:43.640
have been created of that
shared infrastructure.

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:45.657
They're accessing various APIs.

00:19:45.657 --> 00:19:47.240
So I'm showing a
machine learning API,

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:48.615
analytic sort of BigQuery API.

00:19:48.615 --> 00:19:50.240
So those VMs are
still able to do that.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:53.540
Because remember, we set up
that Google private access

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:57.440
in the blue box VPC
when we set up that VPC.

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.830
So all that stuff carries down
to those service projects.

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.500
Now, the other thing that's
really nice about this is we

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:07.460
talked about your
security domain.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.690
And we talked about
your unified policy.

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.900
So in this case, I'm writing
one policy in that blue box.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.830
And when I extend all of those
subnets down to those service

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:19.017
projects, the security policy
actually goes with them.

00:20:19.017 --> 00:20:20.600
So now, you've got--
instead of having

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.550
to build all these
individual VPCs across all

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:24.091
these different
projects, you've been

00:20:24.091 --> 00:20:26.360
able to centralize your VPCs.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:29.180
You've been able to centralize
your security policy.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:32.235
And now, you're able to scale
to hundreds of projects,

00:20:32.235 --> 00:20:34.610
take that infrastructure, push
it down to those projects,

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:37.250
take that security policy,
apply to those projects.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:38.660
And your security
policy, you may

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:41.526
have specific rules that apply
to just the recommendation

00:20:41.526 --> 00:20:43.650
project or just the
personalization project or just

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:45.358
the analytics project,
but you still only

00:20:45.358 --> 00:20:48.305
have one policy that's
written in that blue box.

00:20:48.305 --> 00:20:49.680
And you're applying
certain parts

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.530
of that policy in maybe
the analytics project

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:54.090
or the personalization project
or the recommendation project.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:56.256
But again, when you think
about unifying everything,

00:20:56.256 --> 00:20:57.980
only having to
look at one policy,

00:20:57.980 --> 00:20:59.330
it really simplifies that.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.871
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, especially
from a security perspective,

00:21:01.871 --> 00:21:04.640
management perspective, and
just adding to that scalability.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.610
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yep.

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:08.830
So really, it sort of
continues on that theme.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:11.050
And if you take anything
away from today's talk,

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:13.150
it's really about how do
I simplify my network?

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:15.733
How do I simplify the management
in the operational day-to-day

00:21:15.733 --> 00:21:16.390
functions?

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:18.640
And how can I sort of use
a lot of this flexibility

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:19.690
that Google gives you?

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:21.523
Whether you choose a
very traditional manner

00:21:21.523 --> 00:21:23.650
or you decide to use
some of the functionality

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:26.233
that Google has really created
to really allow you to simplify

00:21:26.233 --> 00:21:28.025
this stuff, that
flexibility is sort of key

00:21:28.025 --> 00:21:29.441
and is something
that we're really

00:21:29.441 --> 00:21:30.590
happy that we can provide.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.830
So again, simplifying
the network,

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:35.410
you're now able to deploy
that VPC across many projects.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.169
So you could have
a single project

00:21:37.169 --> 00:21:39.460
with a shared VPC, another
single project with a shared

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:42.040
VPC, but this allows you to
have just one project, push that

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:44.264
shared VPC to hundreds of them.

00:21:44.264 --> 00:21:46.930
As we talked about before, fewer
networking elements because I'm

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:47.860
building the routers.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.960
I'm building those subnets
in the host project itself

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.177
and in those VPCs.

00:21:52.177 --> 00:21:54.010
Again, I'm not having
sort of that continued

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:55.527
sprawl of things.

00:21:55.527 --> 00:21:57.610
Again, I talked about this,
simplified management.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.651
You have one security
policy that you're managing

00:21:59.651 --> 00:22:02.080
and you can manage this
across hundreds of projects.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Is
there any upper limit

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:04.444
on the number of
service projects

00:22:04.444 --> 00:22:06.940
that you can have under
one shared host project?

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:10.630
RYAN PRZYBYL: So there's no
hard limit that we've seen.

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:13.750
So we've tested up to
5,000 and it runs fine.

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.560
The quota is 100.

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:19.254
So you can do 100 with
nothing, no issues,

00:22:19.254 --> 00:22:21.670
not having to come to Google
and request anything special.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.500
But if you want to go
beyond 100 projects,

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:25.030
you have to put in
a quota increase.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:27.935
But again, in terms of limits,
we've tested up to 5,000

00:22:27.935 --> 00:22:29.712
and had no issues.

00:22:29.712 --> 00:22:31.420
So yeah, we don't
really necessarily know

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:33.430
how big this thing
could potentially scale.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:33.670
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:35.045
Well, if you have
5,000 projects,

00:22:35.045 --> 00:22:37.162
then you're in good hands.

00:22:37.162 --> 00:22:38.620
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
like I did before,

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:41.540
