00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.000
Save it.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.250
STEPHANIE WONG: You really
get that delineation there,

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:27.876
if you don't want to
apply all subnets.

00:24:27.876 --> 00:24:28.666
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:24:28.666 --> 00:24:31.160
You can sort of really get
that granular level you want.

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:33.118
Again, if you want
simplification, you can say,

00:24:33.118 --> 00:24:35.160
I'm just going to
apply all subnets.

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:37.260
If you want sort of to
granularly specify things,

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:38.340
you can do that too.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:39.910
So again, it goes back to
that sort of flexibility.

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:41.040
The way we've engineered
this is to really

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:42.660
be flexible because
as I said earlier,

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:43.826
every business is different.

00:24:43.826 --> 00:24:45.420
Every sort of
networking construct

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:46.680
that we build with
different customers look

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:49.221
slightly different because they
all have different use cases.

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.360
OK.

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:59.860
So now, I can go into
my service project.

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.750
So if I go into
my service project

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:03.670
and I click on VPC
networks, you can

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:05.890
see there's actually no
VPC networks I've created

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:07.330
within the service project.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.090
So I haven't done what I did
in that very first step, where

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:11.260
I've actually built a VPC.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:12.970
I didn't do any of that.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:17.170
All I did was really go in
and share a subnet with this.

00:25:17.170 --> 00:25:19.450
So now, you're going to
see these two options.

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:20.950
So now, you're going
to see networks

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.279
that are shared with this.

00:25:22.279 --> 00:25:23.820
So here, you can
see the two networks

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:25.356
that I've specified here.

00:25:25.356 --> 00:25:26.730
Both the networks
are showing up,

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:29.420
but I've actually only
shared one of them.

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:34.530
So if you go back to
the other environment

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.840
and I go into the
shared VPC menu--

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:47.220
So here, it says network
one, three users.

00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:49.336
So three users are all
my user identities.

00:25:49.336 --> 00:25:50.960
Even though network
one and network two

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:52.920
were both showing up
under that other VPC,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:54.780
I've actually shared
one because I can only

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:56.520
see three users there.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:01.190
So if you look at
this, so I actually

00:26:01.190 --> 00:26:03.550
drill down into the
actual shared service,

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:05.090
the service project.

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:06.830
Again, you'll see
both networks, but you

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:08.222
see there are zero
users in there

00:26:08.222 --> 00:26:10.180
because I haven't actually
shared that network.

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:12.530
So no users can actually
provision any resources

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:13.380
in there.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:15.290
The only network or the
only subnet they can

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:17.222
provision resources
in is this one.

00:26:17.222 --> 00:26:18.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.805
RYAN PRZYBYL: Again,
I could've created

00:26:19.805 --> 00:26:21.400
20 subnets, shared 15 of them.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.307
I could've created 20
and shared all of them.

00:26:23.307 --> 00:26:25.640
Yeah, it sort of depends on
what your specific needs are

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:29.187
and how you're architecting the
VPC components of the network.

00:26:29.187 --> 00:26:30.770
STEPHANIE WONG: So
the number of users

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:34.000
you see here is the number
that are in the service project

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.440
that you share that subnet with?

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:35.740
RYAN PRZYBYL: Correct.

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:38.347
So that service project, I have
three users in that project.

00:26:38.347 --> 00:26:40.180
They happen to be all
be my user identities,

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:42.460
but you could have a whole
ton of users in your project.

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:43.910
Your whole development
team could be there.

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:45.880
So when you share that
resource, it would say,

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.730
maybe, you're sharing
this with 250 users that

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:50.942
are authorized to create
resources in that project.

00:26:50.942 --> 00:26:51.900
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it.

00:26:54.784 --> 00:26:56.700
RYAN PRZYBYL: So again,
very simple to set up.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:59.650
Doesn't take a lot of time.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:01.650
It's really designed to
be very easy and simple.

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:15.400
OK.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:17.950
So now that we've talked about
the VPC construct in itself

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:19.491
and you sort of
built this construct,

00:27:19.491 --> 00:27:21.700
whether you've built it in
a bunch of different VPCs

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:24.992
or you've used sort of
Google's global VPC construct

00:27:24.992 --> 00:27:27.292
or using a shared VPC construct.

00:27:27.292 --> 00:27:28.750
This is stuff that
you've all built

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:31.300
in Google Cloud's environment.

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:33.220
So you still need
to connect to it.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:35.680
So this is connecting from
your on-prem environment

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:38.067
or your data center or
something like that.

00:27:38.067 --> 00:27:40.150
So this is usually the
next logical step of, well,

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.880
how do I get connectivity to
all the stuff that I've built?

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:44.710
So the easiest way
to think about this

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:47.044
is sort of in this
cruciform pattern.

00:27:47.044 --> 00:27:48.460
So on the left
side, you have sort

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.020
of layers of the OSI model.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:53.860
So I have sort of layer 2
options versus layer 3 options.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:56.840
And then across the top,
I have dedicated options,

00:27:56.840 --> 00:27:59.680
meaning I'm directly
connecting with Google's edge.

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:01.480
Or I've got shared
options, which

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:04.060
means I'm connecting to
typically somebody else's

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:05.620
network, which is
then multiplexing

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:07.840
a bunch of customers
together and then pushing

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:09.880
all those customers
at once up to Google.

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:12.400
So there's some other
provider in the middle

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:15.112
in that sort of environment.

00:28:15.112 --> 00:28:16.570
And then you've
got VPN, which I've

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:17.870
sort of put over those top two.

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:20.865
So you can't use VPN with
layer 2 networking options,

00:28:20.865 --> 00:28:22.240
but you're typically
going to use

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:24.646
VPN over the top of
those layer 3 options

00:28:24.646 --> 00:28:26.270
because most people,
as I said earlier,

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:28.750
are building private
address space in there.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.361
So let's start with
those top options

00:28:30.361 --> 00:28:32.860
because that's typically the
way customers start interacting

00:28:32.860 --> 00:28:33.942
with Google Cloud.

00:28:33.942 --> 00:28:35.650
So there's two ways
that they can sort of

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:36.770
do a layer 3 interconnect.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:38.269
When I say layer 3
interconnect, I'm

00:28:38.269 --> 00:28:40.600
basically meaning they're
connecting with our edge

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.130
at layer 3.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:44.640
So you're getting, in
the case of dedicated,

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:46.690
in the upper left-hand
corner, you're actually

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:49.540
connecting to a peering router
on Google's infrastructure

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:51.910
and you're getting all of
Google's net blocks advertised

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:52.409
to you.

00:28:55.032 --> 00:28:56.490
If you go through
the shared route,

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:58.690
this is basically
using your ISP.

00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:01.470
Google's connected to most
ISPs globally in the world.

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:04.120
We're providing all of Google's
net blocks to those ISPs.

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:07.050
Those ISPs are then
advertising them down to you.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:08.610
Now, the big thing
to note here is

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:11.190
there is no SLA around
either of these products.

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:13.244
So the layer 3
interconnects, no SLA.

00:29:13.244 --> 00:29:14.910
So if you're planning
on using this sort

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.460
of connectivity option,
make sure you have some sort

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:19.080
of redundancy built into it.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.637
Connecting into one peering
router on Google directly

00:29:21.637 --> 00:29:23.220
probably is not the
best architecture,

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:26.450
if you're concerned
about high availability.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:28.400
So as I said,
typically customers

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:31.460
are building 10-dot space
in their cloud environments.

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:34.220
So that's private address space.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.184
You can't access that directly
from a public routed edge.

00:29:37.184 --> 00:29:38.600
So the way that
you do that is you

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:40.430
build a VPN over the top of it.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.910
So whether you're using your
ISP to connect to Google

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:46.775
or you're actually connecting
directly to our layer 3 edge,

00:29:46.775 --> 00:29:48.900
you're going to typically
build a VPN over the top.

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:49.910
So this is where you're
going to go and build

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:52.402
VPN gateways within the
VPCs that you set up.

00:29:52.402 --> 00:29:54.860
You're going to build tunnels
to VPN gateways that you have

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:56.649
on-prem or in your data center.

00:29:56.649 --> 00:29:58.190
And that's how you're
going to tunnel

00:29:58.190 --> 00:30:03.074
across the public infrastructure
all those private routes.

00:30:03.074 --> 00:30:05.240
I'll talk a little bit more
next time on the routing

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:08.914
and how this stuff can be
built. But suffice to say,

00:30:08.914 --> 00:30:10.580
you're going to want
to set up some sort

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:13.490
of dynamic routing setup where
you're advertising subnets

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:16.430
across multiple VPNs or
multiple interconnect methods

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:18.430
to give you a high
availability connection.

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:22.420
Because, again, there's no SLA
with these actual services.

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:25.700
Now, the one benefit to the
layer 3 service is right now,

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:26.670
it's free of charge.

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:32.210
You can interconnect to Google's
layer 3 edge for no cost.

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.820
I should note that if you are
doing any dedicated connections

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:37.260
with us, whether it's
layer 3 or layer 2,

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:39.530
everything is done
at the 10 gig layer.

00:30:39.530 --> 00:30:41.510
So if you only
need, say, 500 megs,

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:43.160
you need a gig or
something like that,

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:45.617
typically we're going to
push over to the shared side.

00:30:45.617 --> 00:30:47.700
So it's typically going
to be through some partner

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:52.070
interconnect or through your
ISP or something like that.

00:30:52.070 --> 00:30:54.730
So let's move down the stack
to the layer 2 options.

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:58.630
These tend to be, I would say,
more preferential these days.

00:30:58.630 --> 00:31:00.760
This is really about building.

00:31:09.031 --> 00:31:11.280
So this is really about
building a layer 2 connection.

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:12.990
So these are VLANs
that are being built,

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:15.580
connect your environment
to Google's environment.

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:17.250
So with the dedicated
interconnect,

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:19.666
you're basically connecting--
instead of a peering router,

00:31:19.666 --> 00:31:21.737
you're actually connecting
to a peering fabric.

00:31:21.737 --> 00:31:24.195
So this is our layer 2 device,
just like our peering router

00:31:24.195 --> 00:31:26.850
is our layer 3 device that sits
on the edge of our network.

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:30.060
So you're going to build a
physical interconnect to that.

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:33.030
And then from that, you going to
provision VLANs from that edge

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:37.650
device to cloud routers that
you build in the VPC construct.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.475
The same thing happens
on the partner side.

00:31:39.475 --> 00:31:41.600
In this case, you're going
to connect to a partner.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.350
Google has a whole list
of partners out there.

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:44.820
A good example, a
very popular one

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:46.290
that we work a
lot with customers

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:47.820
is Equinix Cloud Exchange.

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:49.859
Lots of people have
presence in Equinix.

00:31:49.859 --> 00:31:52.400
In this case, you're actually
connecting to Equinix's fabric.

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:54.240
Equinix's fabric is
then connecting directly

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.040
to our peering fabrics.

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:58.650
But you're still really going
through the same process,

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:00.640
where you still have to
get VLANs provisioned,

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:02.601
still have to get that
layer 2 connectivity.

00:32:02.601 --> 00:32:05.100
But again, when you think you
and a whole bunch of customers

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.320
are connecting to
Equinix's peering fabric,

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:09.660
Equinix is then doing
VLAN segregation

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.567
and sending a whole ton of
VLANs to our peering fabric.

00:32:12.567 --> 00:32:14.400
So they're multiplexing
a bunch of customers

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:15.210
on that connection.

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:17.251
STEPHANIE WONG: When is
it necessary for somebody

00:32:17.251 --> 00:32:19.410
to select partner
interconnect versus dedicated?

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:21.576
RYAN PRZYBYL: So there's a
couple of use cases here.

00:32:21.576 --> 00:32:23.130
Normally, there's
two real things.

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:26.340
If you think you're going to
use full 10 gig of connectivity,

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:31.060
it makes more sense to go with
a dedicated method versus using

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:32.520
a shared methodology.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:34.050
Because shared
methodology, you're

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:37.220
competing with other
customers for bandwidth.

00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:39.960
You can't control who is
actually using up bandwidth

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.760
at any given time.

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:44.370
So if you and I are both
connected through Equinix

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:46.620
and you're sending a massive
file the same time I'm

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:48.121
trying to send a
massive file, we're

00:32:48.121 --> 00:32:49.536
basically competing
for bandwidth.

00:32:49.536 --> 00:32:51.549
Now, we try to engineer
with all of our partners

00:32:51.549 --> 00:32:52.840
to prevent that from happening.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:54.960
We do a lot of capacity
planning to say,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:57.090
we should never be
above 50% utilization

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:58.530
on these interconnects.

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:00.420
So the chances of that
happening are slim,

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:02.490
but there's also a
cost component too.

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:04.500
It tends to be more
cost effective to go

00:33:04.500 --> 00:33:05.730
on the dedicated side.

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:06.710
If you're going to
use that full 10

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:08.520
gig port, then going
to the partner side.

00:33:08.520 --> 00:33:11.530
So there's a couple
of trade-offs there.

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:15.690
But typically, if you want
something less than full 10

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.550
gigs or there's some
breakpoint, it's

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:20.700
going to depend on per partner
on where the costs break even

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:21.480
is.

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:25.510
So where the layer 3 dedicated
interconnects were free,

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:28.145
the layer 2 dedicated
interconnects,

00:33:28.145 --> 00:33:29.270
there's a charge for those.

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:30.228
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it.

00:33:30.228 --> 00:33:32.675
RYAN PRZYBYL: So that's
$1,700 a month per port.

00:33:32.675 --> 00:33:35.942
So if you're looking
at a partner version,

00:33:35.942 --> 00:33:37.650
Equinix is going to
charge you something.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:38.600
Google is going to
charge you something.

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:40.040
At some point, it
makes sense just

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:41.380
to go to a dedicated
interconnect.

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:42.255
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:33:42.255 --> 00:33:44.750
Are there any industry best
practices that you've heard of?

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:46.875
RYAN PRZYBYL: So what I
see with a lot of customers

00:33:46.875 --> 00:33:48.582
is that they start
by turning up VPNs

00:33:48.582 --> 00:33:50.540
and then they'll move to
dedicated interconnect

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:52.206
or they'll move to
partner interconnect.

00:33:52.206 --> 00:33:55.160
Now, the great thing about this
is just like the VPC construct,

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:57.090
they're not mutually exclusive.

00:33:57.090 --> 00:33:59.540
And a best practice that
I definitely recommend

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:01.771
is actually using
the VPNs in addition

00:34:01.771 --> 00:34:03.020
to the dedicated interconnect.

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:04.910
So you can start by using VPN.

00:34:04.910 --> 00:34:06.860
You can actually
seamlessly hop over

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:09.120
to a dedicated interconnect
or a partner interconnect.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:11.270
And your traffic will
actually just flip over.

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:11.885
STEPHANIE WONG: Oh, nice.

00:34:11.885 --> 00:34:12.384
Yeah.

00:34:12.384 --> 00:34:14.971
RYAN PRZYBYL: And then you
can leave the VPN as a backup.

00:34:14.971 --> 00:34:16.429
So if something
were to ever happen

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:18.290
with your dedicated interconnect
or your partner interconnect,

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:20.150
the traffic will
automatically failover to VPN.

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:21.858
Since you've already
turned it up anyways

00:34:21.858 --> 00:34:25.440
and it's up and running, why
not just leave it up in place?

00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:28.940
So the later two things
actually do have an SLA.

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:30.980
So the partner
interconnect, the SLA

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:34.179
is actually going be dependent
on the partner that you choose.

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:36.679
From a dedicated interconnect,
depending on the architecture

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:39.750
you use is going to dictate
the SLA that you get.

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:44.659
So if you connect to two
peering fabrics in one metro,

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:47.570
we'll give you 99.9% uptime SLA.

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:50.330
If you connect to two separate
peering fabrics in two

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.300
separate metros, so you have
four connections at that point,

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.336
the SLA goes up to 99.99%.

00:34:55.336 --> 00:34:56.960
So again, depending
on the architecture

00:34:56.960 --> 00:34:58.580
you choose, in terms of
interconnect to Google,

00:34:58.580 --> 00:35:00.140
there's going to
be an SLA behind it

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.030
for both the dedicated
interconnect and the partner

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:02.406
interconnect service.

00:35:02.406 --> 00:35:02.900
STEPHANIE WONG:
Sounds like it could

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:05.720
be appropriate for
hybrid customers as well.

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:06.620
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:08.450
There's a lot of ways
you can mix and match

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:10.257
things in this environment.

00:35:10.257 --> 00:35:11.840
Where this becomes
more challenging is

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:12.600
if you have a
hybrid environment,

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.016
you're running
some stuff in, say,

00:35:14.016 --> 00:35:15.350
AWS and some stuff in Google.

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:17.516
I can't necessarily connect
a dedicated interconnect

00:35:17.516 --> 00:35:20.617
from Google to a AWS
version of this product.

00:35:20.617 --> 00:35:22.700
You actually have to have
something in the middle.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:24.170
So a lot of customers
will deploy a cabinet

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:25.880
with a router or something
like that in Equinix

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:27.620
and then run the
connections into that router

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:29.390
and sort of hairpin
the traffic themselves.

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:29.750
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:30.470
RYAN PRZYBYL: But that
actually gives them

00:35:30.470 --> 00:35:32.210
a cost advantage versus
if they were just

00:35:32.210 --> 00:35:34.670
trying to move traffic
from AWS to Google

00:35:34.670 --> 00:35:36.985
over the public interconnect
that Google has with AWS.

00:35:36.985 --> 00:35:39.110
That's going to actually
cost you a lot more money.

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:41.152
So if you're moving a lot
of data back and forth,

00:35:41.152 --> 00:35:43.776
it's definitely worth looking at
the architecture, where you're

00:35:43.776 --> 00:35:46.280
bringing our dedicated
interconnect service to AWS's

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:49.870
equivalent service and
landing on a router somewhere.

00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:52.120
So that's a quick overview
of sort of all the ways

00:35:52.120 --> 00:35:54.250
that you can interconnect and
how these things can be used

00:35:54.250 --> 00:35:55.550
in conjunction with each other.

00:35:55.550 --> 00:35:58.270
So with that, that's
sort of a wrap for today.

00:35:58.270 --> 00:36:00.479
We covered a bunch of sort
of the foundational stuff.

00:36:00.479 --> 00:36:02.145
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank
you so much, Ryan.

00:36:02.145 --> 00:36:03.340
That was super helpful.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:05.589
Everyone, we're going to be
back in less than a minute

00:36:05.589 --> 00:37:51.602
for live Q&amp;A.

00:37:51.602 --> 00:37:53.810
All right, let's get started
with the first question.

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:56.590
So the first one is, can
I have a shared VPC that

00:37:56.590 --> 00:37:58.180
spans across multiple regions?

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:01.000
And if so, how is
that traffic build?

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:01.780
RYAN PRZYBYL: OK.

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:05.070
So remember the VPC
container automatically

00:38:05.070 --> 00:38:07.420
is going to span across
multiple regions.

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:08.920
So the first part
of that question

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:12.070
is yes, you can definitely have
a shared VPC because a shared

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:13.990
VPC is just taking
that VPC construct

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:16.420
that exists across all
regions and applying it

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:17.914
in the model where it's a host.

00:38:17.914 --> 00:38:20.330
It's in a host project and I'm
sharing it to other things.

00:38:20.330 --> 00:38:23.590
So absolutely, you can do that.

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:24.970
If so, how is traffic build?

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:27.250
Traffic is build at
a per project level.

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:29.770
So when I'm sharing those
resources to another project

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:32.500
and that project is actually
using those resources,

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:34.810
it's actually going to get
build from that project

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:36.768
that I'm sharing it to,
where the VM's running.

00:38:36.768 --> 00:38:39.646
Because the VMs aren't actually
running in the host project.

00:38:39.646 --> 00:38:41.520
They're running in all
those service projects

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:43.799
and the VMs are what is
creating all of the traffic.

00:38:43.799 --> 00:38:46.090
So everything's going to get
build within those service

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:48.718
projects, not necessarily
the host project in itself.

00:38:48.718 --> 00:38:50.630
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, great.

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:53.050
In one of your diagrams,
you show two VMs and a VPC

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.330
with different subnets
in two different regions

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:56.860
being able to talk
to each other.

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:58.960
Does this mean that
a single VPC is

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:01.535
synonymous with a
single broadcast domain?

00:39:01.535 --> 00:39:03.160
RYAN PRZYBYL: So I
wouldn't necessarily

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:05.290
think of it in terms
of a broadcast domain,

00:39:05.290 --> 00:39:11.200
because a broadcast domain is
really a layer 2 construct.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:13.630
So typically, when you think
about a layer 2 broadcast

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.730
domain, what's separating
broadcast domains

00:39:15.730 --> 00:39:18.335
is layer 3 routers.

00:39:18.335 --> 00:39:20.710
In this case, it's not really
a layer 2 broadcast domain.

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:22.810
You can almost think
of it as one big, flat,

00:39:22.810 --> 00:39:24.462
routed environment.

00:39:24.462 --> 00:39:26.170
I'll talk about routing
a little bit more

00:39:26.170 --> 00:39:28.960
in the next Cloud
OnAir that we do.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:31.390
But really, you can think
about every host is actually

00:39:31.390 --> 00:39:34.090
a [INAUDIBLE] router because we
program routes at the VM level.

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:37.090
So it's almost like
this /32 routing domain.

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:42.100
There is no layer 2 broadcast
domain in this environment.

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:45.572
So hopefully that
answers that question.

00:39:45.572 --> 00:39:47.530
STEPHANIE WONG: Can I
enforce security policies

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:49.210
or other
network-related policies

00:39:49.210 --> 00:39:53.337
on communication between
components in the same VPC?

00:39:53.337 --> 00:39:54.420
RYAN PRZYBYL: [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:59.120
So yes, you can.

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:03.710
So within a VPC, you're going
to apply firewall rules.

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:06.230
So for example, I could
have a whole bunch

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:07.370
of subnets in there.

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:10.400
By default, we're actually
going to lock everything down.

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:13.844
Google generally takes
a trust no one policy.

00:40:13.844 --> 00:40:15.260
This is sort of
the way we operate

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:16.680
our own internal network.

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:18.680
So we apply this same
sort of security principle

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:19.490
in the cloud.

00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:22.550
So for example, if you build a
cloud VPC and you build a VM,

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:25.820
and you go try to SSH to it,
you actually can't SSH to it.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:28.460
You actually have to open
the firewall rules to enable

00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:32.712
certain ports, to enable TCP,
to enable ICMP, to enable SSH.

00:40:32.712 --> 00:40:34.170
All this stuff has
to be opened up.

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:36.650
Everything is going be
locked down by default.

00:40:36.650 --> 00:40:40.282
So when you set up
a VPC and you want

00:40:40.282 --> 00:40:41.990
to sort of control
what can talk to what,

00:40:41.990 --> 00:40:44.640
you may build five
different subnets.

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:47.150
And you're going to specify,
OK, I want only this subnet

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:49.910
to talk to only
this other subnet.

00:40:49.910 --> 00:40:52.292
So you can sort of
create that granularity.

00:40:52.292 --> 00:40:53.750
So I talked about
that one customer

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:55.790
that we met with,
where they had very

00:40:55.790 --> 00:40:57.999
specific regional requirements
for their application.

00:40:57.999 --> 00:40:59.831
So we presented that
option to them and say,

00:40:59.831 --> 00:41:01.460
you can really
get very granular.

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:03.860
So you could build
one, big shared VPC

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:05.600
and really build a
lot of firewall rules

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:08.300
around that to
say things in EMEA

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:12.882
can't talk to subnets
in the US or in APAC.

00:41:12.882 --> 00:41:14.840
Again, they decided to
go a different direction

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:17.180
and we liked being able
to offer that flexibility.

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:18.920
But you can definitely
get very granular.

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:21.890
And you can define things
via ports, via protocols,

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:23.330
via subnets.

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:27.320
So the firewall rules are very
granular, in terms of the VPC.

00:41:27.320 --> 00:41:30.620
So maybe you don't want
to allow UDP traffic.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:32.990
OK, then you don't open
it up to allow UDP traffic

00:41:32.990 --> 00:41:35.910
or maybe, heaven forbid,
you don't want TCP traffic.

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:38.090
You're not going have a
lot of communication then,

00:41:38.090 --> 00:41:40.040
but you could actually
lock that off.

00:41:40.040 --> 00:41:43.850
So you have that capability
to get very granular, in terms

00:41:43.850 --> 00:41:46.250
of how you write these
firewall rules to regulate

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:49.640
what can talk to what
within that VPC construct.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:52.580
And the same thing happens when
you talk across multiple VPCs.

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:55.040
You're going to have separate
security policies in each.

00:41:55.040 --> 00:41:57.110
So you may connect
two VPCs together,

00:41:57.110 --> 00:41:59.549
like I showed initially in
that very traditional model.

00:41:59.549 --> 00:42:01.340
If you don't open up
the security policies,

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:03.423
you may have written
security policies in this VPC

00:42:03.423 --> 00:42:06.240
to not allow any external
IP addresses to get to it.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:08.240
So you may have linked
them together with a VPN,

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:09.906
but your security
policies on both sides

00:42:09.906 --> 00:42:11.600
are not allowing
you to communicate.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:14.270
So this goes back to that
complexity and simplifying.

00:42:14.270 --> 00:42:17.000
If you have everything in one,
you can see one security policy

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:17.450
that you're managing.

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:19.190
Here, you're trying to manage
a security policy over here

00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:21.310
and trying to manage another
security policy over there.

00:42:21.310 --> 00:42:22.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:23.570
RYAN PRZYBYL: But to
answer the question, yes.

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:24.646
It's absolutely doable.

00:42:24.646 --> 00:42:26.020
You can be very
granular in this.

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:29.010
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, depends
on how your requirements go.

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:29.510
All right.

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:31.040
We might have time for
one quick last one.

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:32.623
Is it possible to
create a mirror port

00:42:32.623 --> 00:42:35.100
in the Google network of a
server to another server?

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:36.530
RYAN PRZYBYL: So port
mirroring is something

00:42:36.530 --> 00:42:37.446
that we're working on.

00:42:37.446 --> 00:42:39.380
It's not generally
available yet,

00:42:39.380 --> 00:42:42.876
but it's something that we
want to enable as a feature.

00:42:42.876 --> 00:42:45.500
So it is a feature request that
we have in front of our product

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:47.490
team to say, we want to
enable port mirrorings,

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:48.740
specifically on a large scale.

00:42:48.740 --> 00:42:51.190
Because typically when we get
asked for it, a lot of times

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:54.214
it's around security and
various other things.

00:42:54.214 --> 00:42:56.630
So it's something we've asked
our product team to look at,

00:42:56.630 --> 00:42:57.900
to put on the roadmap.

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:00.594
But I don't have a specific
date on when port mirroring is

00:43:00.594 --> 00:43:01.510
going to be available.

00:43:01.510 --> 00:43:02.500
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, no worries.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:02.750
All right.

00:43:02.750 --> 00:43:03.870
Well, thank you, Ryan.

00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:05.970
I think that's about
all the time we have.

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:08.600
Thank you everyone
for joining us today.

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:09.450
Please stick around.

